  "body": {
    "id": "thm:gap-inference",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "The asserted gap-inference theorem does not hold for the frozen projected-score sieve in the stated period-by-period model.  The variance-loss functional is observable and the equality-frontier identity remains valid, but there are bounded finite-support interior laws at which a coordinate declared to satisfy \\(t_{j,P}\\in\\mathcal T_P\\) is instead a nonzero element of \\(\\mathcal T_P^\\perp\\).  Consequently \\(\\zeta_{\\Delta,J_n,P}\\) need not be a tangent-space Riesz approximation, and the displayed asymptotic linearity and Wald coverage do not follow.  A valid gap-inference theorem must replace \\(N'R\\) by the complete period-specific normal vector, differentiate the denominator-corrected \\(\\Psi\\)-covariance, and impose rates controlling the estimated growing-dimensional Riesz map.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "DeltaCmp",
      "tJ",
      "Tang",
      "zetaJ",
      "N",
      "R",
      "Psi",
      "Gamma",
      "Jseq"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:gap-one-step",
      "thm:observable-equivalence",
      "prop:equality-frontier"
    ],
    "justification": "The efficiency comparison becomes empirically interpretable only when sampling uncertainty in the optimized variance loss is quantified.",
    "gap": "Sant'Anna and Xu (2026) construct a Hausman comparison for regular DiD estimators; they do not derive inference for an optimized conditional-versus-constant DDD variance functional.",
    "consumer": "A \\(\\texttt{triplediff}\\) user can report whether an estimated precision gain is separated from zero rather than comparing standard errors informally.",
    "proof_tex": "Use the bounded interior law from the tangent counterexample with \\(T=4\\), \\(\\mathcal K=\\{(2,3)\\}\\), \\(\\mathcal J_{(2,3)}=\\{4,\\infty\\}\\), degenerate \\(X\\), zero conditional gaps, and independent nondegenerate period-two and period-three residual contrasts \\(Z_2=Z_{2;4,\\infty}\\) and \\(Z_3=Z_{3;4,\\infty}\\), scaled to have the same variance.  The corrected observable-equivalence result gives\n\\[\nZ_2,Z_3\\in\\mathcal T_P^\\perp,\n\\qquad E_P[Z_2Z_3]=0,\n\\qquad E_P[Z_2^2]=E_P[Z_3^2]>0.\n\\]\nOn the other hand, the frozen aggregate normal is proportional to \\(Z_2+Z_3\\).  Choose the bounded dictionary coordinate \\(v_1=Z_2-Z_3\\).  Its covariance with the aggregate normal is zero, so the coefficient \\(a_{1,P}\\) in the frozen definition is zero and\n\\[\nt_{1,P}=v_1-E_P[v_1]=Z_2-Z_3.\n\\]\nThis function is nonzero and belongs to \\(\\mathcal T_P^\\perp\\).  At an interior restricted law, \\(\\mathcal T_P\\cap\\mathcal T_P^\\perp=\\{0\\}\\), hence \\(t_{1,P}\\notin\\mathcal T_P\\).  This directly contradicts the load-bearing assertion in the frozen sieve definition.  Independently, the score correction proves that the covariance derivative must be formed from \\(\\Psi\\), not from the raw ratio pseudo-outcome \\(R\\).  Therefore the proposed sieve representer is not the Riesz representer used in the claimed expansion, and no sequence of the stated Gram or approximation assumptions repairs the fact that its coordinates are outside the tangent space.  The counterexample is bounded, has positive cell probabilities, and can be given arbitrarily small independent outcome noise, so it is not a boundary artifact.  The three repairs stated in the narrowed theorem are therefore necessary before a pointwise central limit theorem can be proved.",
